บฐฐ  Quick Start  ฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐบ
ศออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออผ

        Here's how to start flooding ASAP!

        1. Decompress the package which Floods came in.  If you are reading
           this, you should already done that.
        2. You should get the following files:

*       CSWSKCTL VBX        43,648  11-29-96 12:34p CSWSKCTL.VBX
        FILE_ID  DIZ           858  01-23-97  1:00a FILE_ID.DIZ
        FLOODS   DOC        39,407  01-23-97  1:00a FLOODS.DOC
*       FLOODS   EXE       299,952  01-23-97  1:00a floods.exe
*       HACK     DAT         2,491  01-18-97  1:00a HACK.DAT
        WHATS    NEW         4,552  01-23-97  1:00a WHATS.NEW

        <Note: Those files marked with a "*" are required for the proper>
        <execution of the program.                                      >

        3. Delete all the files you do not want.
        4. Start your internet connection.
        5. Execute FLOODS.EXE and have fun!


ษออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออป
บฐฐ  Explanations of Main Menu Commands  ฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐบ
ศออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออผ

        Here's a BRIEF explanation of the menu commands in the Main Menu.

        In the Main Menu (the thing you see immediately when you start the
        program), there are five menus.  They are:

        Bots            Includes commands to control the bots.
        Light Floods    Includes floods that are meant only to be irritating.
        Medium Floods   Includes floods that are meant to flood off people with
                                bad or no flood protection.
        Heavy Floods    Includes floods that are meant to flood off most
                                people.
        Hacks           Includes backdoors for a number of
                                scripts / bots / clients.


        Under the Bots menu, you will find:

        Config          To let you configure the logging type.
        Load            To let you load more bots.
        Stop Loading    To pause the loading process.  Load more bots to
                                resume the loading process.
        Unload          To let you unload loaded bots, so as to save memory.
        Advisor         Starts the Floods Advisor,
        Stop Flooding   To stop flooding on a certain nick.
        Stop All Flooding To stop flooding everyone.
        Change Server   To let you change the server of the bots.
        Change Nick     To let you change the nicks of the bots.
        Raw Command     To let you directly control a bot.
        Network         To let you connect to a Floods Floodnet / Botnet
                                server.
        Exit            To exit the program.


        Under the three flood menus, you will find:

        Action          Floods someone with actions.
        Clientinfo      Floods someone with clientinfos requests.
        CTCP Kill       Floods someone with ALL the CTCP floods.
        DCC Chat        Floods someone with DCC chats.
        DCC Send GarbageFloods someone with DCC sends.
        DCC Kill        Floods someone with both DCC chats and sends.
        Echo            Floods someone with echo requests.
        Finger          Floods someone with finger requests.
        Halibut         Floods someone with halibuts.
        Hurrican        Floods someone with the original Hurrican floods.
        Junk Text       Floods someone by sending him junk messages.
        Notice          Floods someone by sending him junk notices.
        Ping            Floods someone with ping requests.
        Ping Reply      Floods someone by faking ping replys.
        Random          Floods someone with a random flood.
        Sed             Floods someone with seds.
        Sound           Floods someone with sound requests.
        Time            Floods someone with time requests.
        Trout           Floods someone with trouts.
        Tsunami         Floods someone with a tsunami.
        Userinfo        Floods someone with userinfo requests.
        Version         Floods someone with version requests.

        <Note: The differece between the third floods (Light, Medium and  >
        <Heavy) is the interval between floods and the number of floods to>
        <send.                                                            >

        <Note: A flood can also be used to flood a channel.  Use put the>
        <name of the channel instead of the name of a person to flood a >
        <channel.                                                       >
        <E.G:                                                           >
        < #mirc                                                         >


        Under the Hacks menu, you will find:

        Clients         Includes backdoors for clients.
        Scripts/Bots    Includes backdoors for scripts / bots.


ษออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออป
บฐฐ  Explanations of Sub Menus  ฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐบ
ศออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออผ

        When you select certain options in the Main Menu menu commands, a new
        menu will pop up.  This section describes those menus.  Note that for
        ALL sub menus, you must click on the label of the menu (The big button
        at the very top) in order to activate the command requested.  Clicking
        on the button also closes the sub menu.  If you want to close the sub
        menu but not to perform the command, then use ALT-F4.

        When you choose Configuration, you will get a menu with the following:

        Log nothing at all.
        Log everything that is important.
        Log everything.

        These three options determine how sensitive the logging is.

        Choosing "Log nothing at all." will disable logging.
        Choosing "Log everything that is important." will only log important
                events such as logging in and reconnection.
        Choosing "Log everything." will log everything the bot receives in
                addition to what the bot does.


        When you choose Load Bots, you will get a menu with the following:

        Number of clones to load:
        Server to use:
        Port of server:
        Fake e-mail:
        "Real" name:
        Non-sequential nicks

        "Number of clones to load:" refers to the number of bots you wish to
                load or add to already existing ones.
        "Server to use:" refers to the name or IP of the server to connect to.
        "Port of server:" refers to the port of the server you are connecting
                to.  Usually, this value should be 6667, 7000, 6666, 6668 or
                6665, with 6667 being the most common and 6665 the least.
        "Fake e-mail:" refers to the e-mail address you wish to appear from.
                Note that if you have an ident server running, then the remote
                server will take the value from the ident server.
        ""Real" name:" refers to the name you want others to see when they do
                a WHOIS on you.
        "Non-sequential nicks" when checked, will mean that the bots will not
                have nicks with their numbers-1 appended.
                E.G:
                If you have it off, bot one may have a nick like "Dav0" and
                bot two one like "Dav1"
                If you have it on, bot one may have a nick like "Dav" and bot
                two "Fat"


        When you choose Unload Bots, you will get a menu with the following:

        Number of clones to unload:
        Quit gracefully
        Quit without informing server

        "Number of clones to unload:" refers to the number of bots to unload
                starting from the BACK.
                E.G:
                If you have 5 bots on and you unload 3 then only bots 1 and 2
                will remain.

        "Quit gracefully" and "Quit without informing server" are used to
        determine how the bot quits.

        By using "Quit gracefully", the bot will first inform the server that
                it is quitting before disconnecting.
        By using "Quit without informing server", the bot will just
                disconnect.  This will cause the bot to appear to be still on.
                Until the server pings the bot, the resources dedicated to the
                bot by the server will be lost.


        When you choose Advisor, you will get a menu with the following:

        Some questions, options.

        Answer all the questions truthfully and if you do not know any of the
        first 3 answers, put in the second smallest value.  If you do not know
        any of the least 2 answers, put in the biggest value.  Note that the
        reply from the Advisor MAY NOT be correct.  You should add in a bit of
        common sense.  If your flood fails, try increasing the "dosage" and if
        it got your bots off too often, try decreasing it.  If that still does
        not work, then e-mail me and give as much information about the
        situation as possible.  Of course, this would mean that you will not
        be able to flood the victim then, but then, at least you get to learn.


        When you choose Change Server, you will get a menu with the following:

        From ? to ?
        Change server to:
        Server to use:
        Fake e-mail:
        "Real" name:
        Non-sequential nicks
        
        "From ? to ?" refers to the start and end of the bots to perform the
                action.  The first "?" refers to the start bot number and the
                other refers to the end bot number.
                E.G:
                "From 1 to 3" means that clones 1, 2 and 3 will perform the
                action.

        "Change server to:" refers to the name or IP of the server to connect to.

        For explanations of "Port of server:", "Fake e-mail:", ""Real" name:"
                and "Non-sequential nicks" refer to previous explanations.


        When you choose any flood, you will get a menu with the following:

        Flood Type:
        Flood:
        From ? to ?

        "Flood Type:" is already set and refers to the flood you are going to
                release.
        "Flood:" refers to the person / channel to flood.

        For explanations of "From ? to ?", refer to previous explanation.


        When you choose Change Nick, you will get a menu with the following:

        From ? to ?
        Change nick to:

        "Change nick to:" refers to the nick to change to.

        For explanations of "From ? to ?", refer to previous explanation.

        <Note: If in "From ? to ?", both the "?" are the same, then the bot>
        <will change to a non-sequential nick.                             >
        <E.G:                                                              >
        <From 1 to 1, new nick = "David"                                   >
        <Bot 1 will change nick to "David"                                 >


        When you choose Raw Command, you will get a menu with the following:

        From ? to ?
        Command to send:

        "Command to send:" refers to the command to send.  Note that this
                command is in the form of direct server commands, so to send
                a message, you will use:
                PRIVMSG <NICK / CHANNEL> <MESSAGE>
                You are not advised to use this option if you are unfamiliar
                with direct server commands.  Note that if you want to perform
                more than one command, you can seperate them with a "|".
                Also, having a number in front of a command will make the bots
                perform the action for that number of times.  Note that this
                timer CANNOT be nested, so "2 2 PRIVMSG #mirc Hello" will NOT
                send 4 "Hello"s to #mirc, but will just give an error.
                E.G:
                If you have bots 1 to 3 performing "2 PRIVMSG #mirc Hello"
                then the sequence of actions will be:

                Bot 1 -> PRIVMSG #mirc Hello
                Bot 2 -> PRIVMSG #mirc Hello
                Bot 3 -> PRIVMSG #mirc Hello
                Bot 1 -> PRIVMSG #mirc Hello
                Bot 2 -> PRIVMSG #mirc Hello
                Bot 3 -> PRIVMSG #mirc Hello

                If you have bots 1 to 3 performing "PRIVMSG #mirc Hello |
                PRIVMSG #mirc Hello again!" then the sequence of actions will
                be:

                Bot 1 -> PRIVMSG #mirc Hello
                Bot 2 -> PRIVMSG #mirc Hello
                Bot 3 -> PRIVMSG #mirc Hello
                Bot 1 -> PRIVMSG #mirc Hello again!
                Bot 2 -> PRIVMSG #mirc Hello again!
                Bot 3 -> PRIVMSG #mirc Hello again!

                <Note: Due to the fact that some bots may be faster than    >
                <others and that some bots may be tied down performing other>
                <commands, the sequence may NOT be strictly followed.       >

        For explanations of "From ? to ?", refer to previous explanation.


        When you choose Network, you will get a menu with the following:

        Connect / Disconnect
        Connected
        Hop list
        And a dropdown menu with:
        Chat
        Flood
        Sort Lists
        Find an IP

        "Connect / Disconnect" is a button that displays either "Connect" or
                "Disconnect" depending whether you are connected, as a client,
                or not.  Use this button to disconnect or connect.
        "Connected" contains a list of ALL the clients / servers connected to
                the network.
        "Hop list" contains a list of ALL the clients / servers connected to
                the network with the route messages will take to reach them.
                E.G:
                111.111.111.111|666.666.666.666|222.222.222.222
                means that if you want to send a message to 222.222.222.222
                you need to send the message to 111.111.111.111 which will
                send the message to 666.666.666.666 which will in turn send
                the message to 222.222.222.222.
        Chat            Start the network chatting menu.
        Flood           Starts the network flood menu, which will let you
                                select a flood for everyone to flood the
                                victim with.
        Sort Lists      Sorts both your list of network clients / servers.
        Find an IP      Finds either an IP or a hop list in your lists.
                        E.G:
                        If you want to find IP 111.111.111.111 you type in
                        "111.111.111.111".  If you want to find the message
                        route to 111.111.111.111, you type "route
                        111.111.111.111". If you already know the route and
                        just want to have a look at it, type in
                        "666.666.666.666|111.111.111.111", whereby
                        666.666.666.666 is the route.  If you want to list all
                        the clients whose route contain 111.111.111.111 you
                        type in "path 111.111.111.111", note that this list
                        will include "111.111.111.111" itself.

        <Note: If you choose Network without being the client to any       >
        <connection, then you will be asked whether you want to connect to >
        <anyone.  You need to connect to someone or have someone connect to>
        <you before you can chat over the network or request for a network >
        <flood.                                                            >


        When you choose Chat, you will get a menu with the following:

        A list box
        A text box

        The list box will list all the conversation that took place and any
                other network message such as someone requesting for a flood.
                Note that if you want to clear the list, just double click
                on any line in the list box.
        The text box will allow you to chat.  If you just type a message in,
                then the message will reach everyone on the network.  If you
                use "/MSG <IP> <MESSAGE>", whereby <IP> is the address of
                someone on the network and <MESSAGE> is the message you want
                to send, the message will only reach <IP>.  It is a good idea
                to inform others via chat before starting a network flood.


        When you choose Flood, you will get a menu with the following:

        Flood level
        One dropdown list
        Flood:

        "Flood level" refers to the intensity of the flood, Light, Medium or
                Heavy.
        The dropdown list is used to let you select the flood to request.
        "Flood:" refers to the nick to flood.


        When you choose Hacks, you will get a menu with the following:

        One dropdown list
        Bot number:
        Send to:

        The dropdown list is used to let you select the backdoor to use.
        "Bot number:" refers to the bot to perform the backdoor.
        "Send to:" refers to the person to receive the backdoor.

        <Note: ALL backdoors are stored in HACK.DAT, which you are edit   >
        <yourself to reflect changes or additions.  The format of HACK.DAT>
        <is:                                                              >
        <                                                                 >
        <-----<Client/Script>-----                                        >
        <followed by the hacks.                                           >
        <                                                                 >
        <The hacks themselves are in the format:                          >
        <                                                                 >
        < <Name of hack>:<Command>                                        >
        <                                                                 >
        <The <Command> format is similiar to the format for raw commands. >
        <                                                                 >
        <There are 3 additional tags for <Command>.  They are:            >
        <                                                                 >
        < $1 -> Refers to the person to send the backdoor to.             >
        < #  -> Refers to the channel to send the backdoor to.  If there  >
        <               is no "#" in front, then one will be added.       >
        < $? -> The format of this is: $?="<Message>".  Whereby <Message> >
        <               refers to a prompt to ask for a command.          >
        <                                                                 >
        < For more information, refer to the copy of HACK.DAT embedded in >
        < this document.                                                  >


ษออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออป
บฐฐ  Floodnet / Botnet  ฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐฐบ
ศออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออผ

        There is now a new way to flood.  It normally consists of an eggdrop
        or some other server which is permanantly on IRC.  If someone wishes
        to join the network, he dcc chats or raw connect to the eggdrop.  When
        anyone on the network decides he doesn't like the nick of a victim, he
        can use a command, such as !pkill, and EVERYONE on the network with
        Floodnet / Botnet compatibility will start flooding the victim.  This
        way, using conventional flood protection, Ignore after 5-10
        messages / CTCPs, will still get the victim off.

        Floods has its own built-in Floodnet / Botnet.  When you wish to
        connect to someone, just go to the main menu and select Bot->Network.
        Floods will then prompt you whether you want to connect and so on.
        Alternatively, you can go to the Network sub menu and click on the
        Connect button.  If you can connected, the connect button will change
        to disconnect.  Refer to "Explanations of Main Menu Commands" and
        "Explanations of Sub Menus" for more information.

        However, there is ONE difference between conventional Floodnet /
        Botnet and Floods' Floodnet / Botnet.  For Floods' Floodnet / Botnet,
        you DO NOT NEED A SERVER.  Everyone on the network can be a server and
        a client.  However, you can only be the client in ONE connection.  For
        example:

                  A -<- B -<- C
                        |
                        ^
                        |                    A -<- B means that B is a client
                  F ->- D -<- E              connected to A.

        In this sample network, there is no prominent server, except maybe for
        A.  In this example, if B decides to flood someone, B will send the
        command string to all DIRECTLY connected to himself: A, C and D.  Then
        those 3 will send the command string to those DIRECTLY connected to
        them, EXCEPT B.  This way, ALL the clients / servers get the command.

        The only two drawbacks of this system of networking is that if B
        disconnects, A and C will be out of the network.  However, the good
        news is that Floods will automatically make C and D connect to A.
        The other drawback is that if D is desperately lagged, then when B
        wants to flood someone, E and F may not get the message fast enough.
        But then, this way, as there is no server, no single one of the clients
        / servers will get too lag due to the network.  Of course, if you can
        get me a FAST PERMANENT site for an eggdrop, I would gladly set one
        server up.  Of course, other than the server, I may use the site for
        other stuff :).


