ศออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออผ

     This new section is devoted to announcements, interesting stuff, tips,
     tricks, questions, etc. on Floods and Floods LITE.  Feel free to send me
     e-mail on additions for this section.

        - Announcements -

        -> You can now get the latest copy of Floods at
           http://www.post1.com/~h-master

        -> Note that my e-mail has changed!

        -> I really need a site where you can run an eggdrop, so as to set up
           a permanent Floodnet / Botnet server.  If you send me one that is
           fast and works, I will upgrade you to supporter status.  Also, if
           you require payment, we can always come to an agreement.


        - Interesting Stuff / Tips / Tricks -

        -> Pros and Cons of different types of networking.
           - If you are in the middle of the network, such as B and D,

                  A -<- B -<- C
                        |
                        ^
                        |                    A -<- B means that B is a client
                  F ->- D -<- E              connected to A.

             your messages will be spread to the most people in the least
             time.  This mean that if you want to flood someone, more people
             will be able to flood with you then if you are at the end of a
             network, such as F, E, A and C.  However, you must have a fast
             connection or else all the messages passing through you to get
             to the others will lag you.

           - I am going to introduce 3 types of networking to you.  I am not
             sure whether they have any "accepted" name and will make them
             up as we go along.

             The first type is linear networking:

                  A -<- B -<- C              A -<- B means that B is a client
                                             connected to A.

             This, in my opinion is the WORST way to form a network.  If any
             client / server in the middle decides to disconnect, the entire
             network breaks up into 2.  Worst still, if C wants to flood
             someone, by the time the message reaches A, C may already have
             started flooding.  This means that it's harder to flood together.
             Imagine if the network consists of 10 clients / servers.  If the
             first wants to flood, he may already stop before the last one
             starts flooding!

             The second type is server-based networking:

                  A ->- B -<- C              A -<- B means that B is a client
                        |                    connected to A.
                        ^
                        |
                        D

             In this case, B is the server.  This is a better type of
             networking than linear networking because if any CLIENT
             disconnects, the network is still in one "piece".  Also, the
             maximum number of computers any message has to pass through is
             one, excluding the sender and receiver.  However, there are some
             requirements.  Firstly, B must be VERY fast especially if it's
             a large network, otherwise, the entire network gets lagged and
             messages cannot get around fast enough.  Secondly, if B does
             down, the entire network is gone.  Not in 2 "pieces" as in linear
             networking, but totally gone.

             The last type is client-based networking:

                  A -<- B -<- C
                        |
                        ^
                        |                    A -<- B means that B is a client
                  F ->- D -<- E              connected to A.

             In my opinion, this is the best of both worlds.  It isn't as slow
             as linear networking, it doesn't require any particular
             client / server to be super fast and it doesn't break up totally
             if any client / server quits.  However, as it is still more or
             less linear-based, it is slower than server-based networking
             (assuming it takes one unit of time for a message to be passed
             from any one client / server to another) and it requires that
             SOME of the clients / servers be reasonaly fast, between linear
             and server-based requirements.


        - Frequently Asked Questions -

        -> Q: When I start Floods, it says that I do not have some of the
              files.  Where can I get them?
           A: You should be able to get ALL the files required by Floods at my
              homepage.  If you can't, leave me a message.

        -> Q: Windows reported that I have a "device unavailible" error.  What
              should I do?
           A: Your copy of winsock.dll is not compatible with Floods.  Get a
              new one.  Note that the copy of winsock I am using is the one
              supplied with Windows 95.

        -> Q: When I start the program, Windows 95 says that the language file
              is missing and refuses to run the program.
           A: This is quite a common problem.  There are 2 solutions I can
              think of.  They are:

              1. Install Visual Basic 4.0.  Once installed, VB4 will set the
                 language.dll option in your win.ini.
              2. Set the language.dll option yourself.  An easy way to do this
                 is to run setup, change the language to something else.  Then
                 save the new settings.  After that, if you want the language
                 to be the old one, then run setup again and change it back.
                 Note that this is not guranteed to work, but it worked for
                 me.


